# Melanin-Compensated Non-Invasive Glucometer

## Overview

A non-invasive glucose estimation prototype that uses infrared optical sensing and melanin compensation to reduce the influence of skin pigmentation on glucose estimation.

## Problem Statement

Conventional glucose monitoring generally requires blood sampling. Optical non-invasive approaches can be affected by variations in skin pigmentation. This project explores a pigmentation-aware approach to optical glucose estimation.

## Objectives

- Develop a non-invasive optical sensing prototype.
- Acquire optical signals using a 940 nm infrared source.
- Process signals using an ESP32 microcontroller.
- Estimate skin pigmentation using a melanin index.
- Apply melanin compensation to optical measurements.
- Display real-time measurements and signal information.

## Hardware Components

- ESP32 microcontroller
- 940 nm infrared LED
- BPW34 photodiode
- MCP6002 operational amplifier
- Supporting electronic components and optical sensing enclosure

## Key Features

- Non-invasive fingertip optical sensing
- Melanin index estimation
- Melanin-compensated glucose estimation
- Comparison of raw and compensated readings
- Real-time monitoring dashboard
- Signal quality and perfusion information
- Heart-rate display
- Optical signal graphs

## Working Principle

1. The infrared LED illuminates the fingertip.
2. The photodetector captures the resulting optical signal.
3. The analog signal is conditioned and acquired by the microcontroller.
4. The system analyses optical measurements and estimates pigmentation using a melanin index.
5. A compensation algorithm adjusts the measurements to account for pigmentation-related variations.
6. The system displays the estimated glucose value and supporting parameters on the monitoring dashboard.

## Technology Stack

- Embedded hardware: ESP32
- Optical sensing: 940 nm infrared LED and BPW34 photodiode
- Signal conditioning: MCP6002 op-amp
- Firmware development: Arduino IDE / Embedded C-C++
- Signal processing: Optical signal analysis and melanin compensation
- Visualization: Real-time monitoring dashboard

## Prototype Demonstration

A working prototype demonstration video is included in this repository.

## Applications

- Research into non-invasive glucose monitoring
- Pigmentation-aware optical sensing
- Optical biosensing and signal processing

## Future Scope

- Calibration and validation against reference glucose measurements
- Testing across diverse skin pigmentation levels
- Improving signal accuracy and repeatability
- Miniaturization and development of a portable device
- Further algorithm optimization

## Disclaimer

This is an experimental research prototype and is not a clinically validated medical device. Its displayed glucose values are estimates and should not be used for medical diagnosis or treatment decisions.

## Project Status

Working prototype – Research and development stage.
